\title{Destructive Verification:\\ Verdicts with a Residual State}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Verification is conventionally modelled as a predicate: a checker consumes an
object and returns a Boolean. This model silently assumes that the object
survives the check, an assumption violated by destructive material testing,
state-collapsing measurement, and single-use consumable certificates. We model
verification instead as a \emph{state transition} $t : D \to \B \times D$ on a
type $D$ of \emph{dishes}, returning both a \emph{verdict} and a \emph{residual
dish}, and use the model to separate --- by theorems rather than by
stipulation --- three notions the predicate model conflates: \emph{nondestructive}
tests (the dish is returned unchanged), \emph{reversible} tests (the residue
map is a bijection) and \emph{repeatable} tests (re-running on the residue
reproduces the verdict).

We prove that nondestructiveness implies both reversibility and repeatability,
that no further implication holds, and that the separation persists at the level
of closure properties: tests form a monoid under sequential composition in which
certificates form a commutative idempotent submonoid ordered by inclusion of
accepted sets, while repeatable tests are not closed under composition at all.
Counting gives $(2n)^n$ tests, $2^n\,n!$ reversible tests, and only $2^n$
certificates on $n$ dishes. Iterating a test on its own residue produces a
\emph{transcript}. We prove \emph{transcript rigidity} --- a transcript constant
on its first $n = |D|$ entries is constant forever --- and show it is sharp; we
characterise the realisable transcripts exactly as the eventually periodic
streams with $\text{preperiod} + \text{period} \le n$, with a strict hierarchy
at every level; we prove that every test stabilises, its residue map becoming a
bijection on a stabilised core, so that destruction is confined to the
transient; and we prove that observational equivalence is decided by $n$ runs,
combining a minimal-recurrence analysis of orbits with the Fine--Wilf
periodicity lemma from combinatorics on words. No hardness label of any kind is
assigned to any class.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} destructive testing, state transition, verification,
transcript, eventual periodicity, Fine--Wilf periodicity lemma, transformation
monoid, state complexity, orbit combinatorics.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The missing residue}

Let $D$ be a set of objects and $P \subseteq D$ a property. The classical model
of verification is a function $D \to \B$, where $\B = \{\tru, \fls\}$, ideally
with $t^{-1}(\tru) = P$. Almost the entire theoretical apparatus around
verification is phrased over this model.

The model encodes a physical hypothesis that is rarely stated: \emph{checking is
free with respect to the object}. After the check, the object is available,
unchanged, for another check. This is true of a mathematical proof and false of
almost everything else. Tensile testing severs the specimen. Sterility testing
consumes the sample. A projective quantum measurement collapses the state. A
one-time authentication token is spent by its own verification.

The failure is not that the predicate model gives wrong answers; it is that it
cannot pose the questions. ``May I run this battery in the other order?'' ``How
many identical verdicts entitle me to stop testing?'' ``Does this test destroy
the property it is checking?'' None of these is expressible about a function
$D \to \B$.

\subsection{The model}

We replace the predicate by a state transition. All of the content of the paper
follows from this single change of type.

\begin{definition}[Test]
A \textbf{test} on a type $D$ of \textbf{dishes} is a function
\[ t : D \longrightarrow \B \times D. \]
We write $v_t(d) = \pi_1(t(d))$ for the \textbf{verdict} and
$r_t(d) = \pi_2(t(d))$ for the \textbf{residue}, so $t(d) = (v_t(d), r_t(d))$.
Two tests are equal iff they agree on all verdicts and all residues.
\end{definition}

The predicate model is recovered as the special case $r_t = \id$. The point of
the paper is to measure how special that case is. Throughout, $n$ denotes $|D|$
when $D$ is finite, $f\iter{k}$ denotes the $k$-fold iterate of $f : D \to D$
(with $f\iter{0} = \id$), and $\B$ denotes the Booleans.

\section{The taxonomy}

\begin{definition}\label{def:taxonomy}
Let $t$ be a test on $D$.
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item $t$ is \textbf{nondestructive} (a \emph{certificate check}) if
    $r_t(d) = d$ for all $d$.
  \item $t$ is \textbf{reversible} if $r_t : D \to D$ is a bijection.
  \item $t$ is \textbf{repeatable} if $v_t(r_t(d)) = v_t(d)$ for all $d$.
  \item $t$ is \textbf{destructive} if it is not nondestructive.
  \item $t$ \textbf{decides} a property $P \subseteq D$ if
    $v_t(d) = \tru \iff d \in P$ for all $d$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

Informally: nondestructive means \emph{nothing happened to the dish};
reversible means \emph{something happened but no information was lost};
repeatable means \emph{the answer is stable under re-asking}.

\begin{theorem}[Certificates are reversible]\label{thm:nd-rev}
If $t$ is nondestructive then $t$ is reversible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$r_t = \id_D$, which is a bijection.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Certificates are strongly repeatable]\label{thm:nd-rep}
If $t$ is nondestructive then $v_t\big(r_t\iter{k}(d)\big) = v_t(d)$ for all
$k \in \N$ and $d \in D$. In particular $t$ is repeatable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$. For $k=0$ this is trivial. For the step,
$r_t\iter{k+1}(d) = r_t\iter{k}(r_t(d)) = r_t\iter{k}(d)$ since $r_t(d) = d$,
and the induction hypothesis applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Repeatability propagates along orbits]\label{thm:rep-iter}
If $t$ is repeatable then $v_t(r_t\iter{k}(d)) = v_t(d)$ for all $k$ and $d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$, using $r_t\iter{k+1}(d) = r_t(r_t\iter{k}(d))$, applying
repeatability at the point $r_t\iter{k}(d)$, then the induction hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A repeatable test preserves what it decides]\label{thm:invariant}
Let $t$ be repeatable and suppose $t$ decides $P$. Then $P(r_t(d)) \iff P(d)$
for every dish $d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$P(r_t(d)) \iff v_t(r_t(d)) = \tru \iff v_t(d) = \tru \iff P(d)$, using the
deciding hypothesis twice and repeatability once.
\end{proof}

A nondestructive test, by contrast, preserves \emph{every} property, which is a
precise way of saying it does nothing at all.

\section{Strictness: no further implication holds}

Two dishes suffice. Take $D = \B$ and define
\[ \flip(d) = (\tru,\, \lnot d), \qquad
   \readflip(d) = (d,\, \lnot d), \qquad
   \burn(d) = (\tru,\, \fls). \]

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:flip}
$\flip$ is reversible and repeatable but destructive.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Its residue is negation, an involution, hence a bijection; its verdict is
constantly $\tru$, hence repeatable; and $r(\tru) = \fls \neq \tru$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:readflip}
$\readflip$ is reversible but not repeatable.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Its residue is negation, a bijection. But
$v(r(\tru)) = v(\fls) = \fls \neq \tru = v(\tru)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:burn}
$\burn$ is repeatable but not reversible, and is destructive.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Its verdict is constant, hence repeatable; its residue is constant, hence not
injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict taxonomy]\label{thm:strict}
On a two-element dish type there is a reversible non-repeatable test, a
repeatable non-reversible test, and a test that is both reversible and
repeatable yet destructive. Hence no containment holds among the three classes
beyond Theorems~\ref{thm:nd-rev} and~\ref{thm:nd-rep}.
\end{theorem}

The test $\readflip$ loses no information and yet its second run contradicts its
first: a minimal combinatorial shadow of a measurement that is
information-preserving but disturbing. Conversely $\burn$ is repeatable
\emph{precisely because} its destruction is total and uniform.

\section{Sequential composition: the verification monoid}

\begin{definition}[Sequential composition]
For tests $t_1, t_2$ on $D$,
\[ (t_1 \cdot t_2)(d) = \big(v_{t_1}(d) \wedge v_{t_2}(r_{t_1}(d)),\;
   r_{t_2}(r_{t_1}(d))\big), \]
and the \textbf{trivial certificate} is $\mathbf{1}(d) = (\tru, d)$.
\end{definition}

Thus $v_{t_1\cdot t_2} = v_{t_1} \wedge (v_{t_2}\circ r_{t_1})$ and
$r_{t_1 \cdot t_2} = r_{t_2}\circ r_{t_1}$.

\begin{theorem}[Monoid]\label{thm:monoid}
$(\test(D), \cdot, \mathbf 1)$ is a monoid.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Compare verdicts and residues separately. Residues compose as
$r_{t_3}\circ r_{t_2}\circ r_{t_1}$ under either bracketing; verdicts are
$v_1(d)\wedge v_2(r_1 d)\wedge v_3(r_2r_1 d)$ under either bracketing, by
associativity of $\wedge$. The unit laws follow from $v_{\mathbf 1} = \tru$ and
$r_{\mathbf 1} = \id$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closure]\label{thm:closure}
Certificates are closed under composition, and so are reversible tests.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Compositions of identities are identities; compositions of bijections are
bijections.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Certificates commute]\label{thm:comm}
If $t_1, t_2$ are nondestructive then $t_1 \cdot t_2 = t_2 \cdot t_1$: equal
verdicts on every dish and equal residues on every dish.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both composites have residue $\id$. Since $r_1(d) = r_2(d) = d$, the composite
verdicts are $v_1(d)\wedge v_2(d)$ and $v_2(d)\wedge v_1(d)$, equal by
commutativity of $\wedge$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Destructive tests do not commute]\label{thm:noncomm}
There exist tests $t_1, t_2$ on a two-element dish type with $t_1$
nondestructive, $t_2$ destructive, and $t_1\cdot t_2 \neq t_2\cdot t_1$;
moreover the composites differ in the \emph{verdict}, not merely in the residue.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $t_1(d) = (d,d)$ and $t_2 = \burn$. Then $(t_1\cdot t_2)(\tru)$ has verdict
$\tru \wedge \tru = \tru$, while $(t_2 \cdot t_1)(\tru)$ has verdict
$\tru \wedge v_{t_1}(\fls) = \fls$.
\end{proof}

Since the composites differ in the verdict function alone, the order of a
battery containing a destructive test is \emph{observable to the verifier}. A
battery of $m$ tests can exhibit up to $m!$ distinct behaviours; a battery of
certificates exhibits exactly one.

\subsection{The algebra of certificates}

\begin{theorem}[Certificates form a Boolean semilattice]\label{thm:semilattice}
For nondestructive $c, c_1, c_2$:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,label=(\arabic*)]
  \item $c\cdot c = c$;
  \item $v_{c_1\cdot c_2}(d) = v_{c_1}(d)\wedge v_{c_2}(d)$;
  \item $c_1 \cdot c_2 = c_1 \iff \{d : v_{c_1}(d)\}\subseteq\{d: v_{c_2}(d)\}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1) and (2) are immediate from $r = \id$. For (3): if $c_1\cdot c_2 = c_1$ then
evaluating verdicts at an accepted $d$ gives $v_{c_1}(d)\wedge v_{c_2}(d) =
v_{c_1}(d) = \tru$, forcing $v_{c_2}(d) = \tru$; conversely inclusion collapses
the conjunction to $v_{c_1}$ pointwise, and both residues are the identity.
\end{proof}

So the certificate sub-poset is exactly the Boolean lattice $2^D$ with
$\mathbf 1$ on top, and the verification monoid is a genuinely non-commutative
extension of it.

\begin{theorem}[Destruction breaks idempotence]\label{thm:idem}
There is a destructive test $t$ with $t\cdot t \neq t$; indeed
$\readflip\cdot\readflip$ and $\readflip$ differ in verdict at $\tru$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Reversibility equals restorability}

\begin{theorem}[Reversible $=$ restorable]\label{thm:restorable}
Let $D$ be finite and $t$ a test. Then $t$ is reversible if and only if there is
a test $u$ with $t\cdot u$ nondestructive.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $r_t$ is a bijection with inverse $g$, take
$u(d) = (\tru, g(d))$; then $r_{t\cdot u} = g\circ r_t = \id$.
($\Leftarrow$) If $r_u\circ r_t = \id$ then $r_t$ is injective, and an injective
self-map of a finite set is a bijection.
\end{proof}

Thus ``no information lost'' and ``can be undone by a follow-up test'' are the
same condition. The burn test is restorable by no follow-up whatsoever; the flip
test is restorable, though not by itself.

\subsection{Repeatability is not compositional}

\begin{theorem}[Repeatable tests are not closed under composition]\label{thm:rep-notclosed}
There exist repeatable tests $t_1, t_2$ on a two-element dish type --- with
$t_1$ even a certificate --- such that $t_1\cdot t_2$ is not repeatable.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $t_1(d) = (d,d)$, a certificate, and $t_2 = \flip$, repeatable by
Proposition~\ref{prop:flip}. Their composite has verdict $v(d) = d\wedge\tru = d$
and residue $r(d) = \lnot d$, i.e.\ it is $\readflip$, not repeatable by
Proposition~\ref{prop:readflip}.
\end{proof}

Certificates form a submonoid; reversible tests form a submonoid; repeatable
tests form neither. This is a closure-property separation, strictly finer than
the pointwise separation of Theorem~\ref{thm:strict}.

\section{Counting}

\begin{theorem}[Census]\label{thm:census}
Let $|D| = n$. There are exactly $(2n)^n$ tests on $D$, exactly $2^n$
nondestructive tests, and exactly $2^n\cdot n!$ reversible tests.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A test is a function $D \to \B\times D$ and $|\B\times D| = 2n$. Sending a
certificate to its verdict function is a bijection onto $D\to\B$, since the
residue is determined. Sending a reversible test to the pair (verdict function,
residue permutation) is a bijection onto $(D\to\B)\times\mathfrak{S}_D$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For $n\ge 2$, $2^n < (2n)^n$, with ratio $n^n$: certificates are an
exponentially small minority of tests, and are exactly the slice of the
reversible tests on which the permutation is trivial.
\end{corollary}

\section{Transcripts, rigidity, and destruction depth}

\begin{definition}[Transcript]
The \textbf{transcript} of a test $t$ on a dish $d$ is
$T_{t,d}(k) = v_t\big(r_t\iter{k}(d)\big)$, $k\in\N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$t$ is repeatable if and only if every transcript of $t$ is constant.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:rep-iter} for one direction; take $k=1$ at an arbitrary dish
for the other.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Orbit lemma]\label{lem:orbit}
Let $|D| = n$, $f : D\to D$, $d\in D$. For every $m$ there is $j < n$ with
$f\iter{m}(d) = f\iter{j}(d)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Among the $n+1$ values $f\iter{0}(d),\dots,f\iter{n}(d)$ two coincide, giving a
recurrence $f\iter{i+p}(d) = f\iter{i}(d)$ with $p > 0$ and $i+p \le n$. Then
$f\iter{i}(d)$ is a periodic point of period $p$, so for $m\ge i$ we may reduce
the excess modulo $p$: $f\iter{m}(d) = f\iter{\,i+((m-i)\bmod p)\,}(d)$, with
$i + ((m-i)\bmod p) < i+p \le n$. For $m < i$ take $j = m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Transcript rigidity]\label{thm:rigid}
Let $|D| = n$. If $T_{t,d}(j) = T_{t,d}(0)$ for all $j < n$, then
$T_{t,d}(m) = T_{t,d}(0)$ for all $m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Given $m$, Lemma~\ref{lem:orbit} supplies $j < n$ with $r_t\iter{m}(d) =
r_t\iter{j}(d)$; apply $v_t$ and use the hypothesis at $j$.
\end{proof}

Define the \textbf{destruction depth} of $(t,d)$ to be the least $m$ with
$T_{t,d}(m)\neq T_{t,d}(0)$, or $\infty$ if none exists. Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid}
says the depth is either infinite or $< n$; the bound is attained at every value
below $n$.

\begin{definition}[Fuse test]
For $k\in\N$ define $\fuse_k$ on the dish type $\{0,\dots,k+1\}$ by
$\fuse_k(j) = \big([\,j\le k\,],\ \min(j+1, k+1)\big)$: the dish advances one
notch per run and sticks at the last notch, where the verdict flips.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:fuse}
$T_{\fuse_k,\,0}(m) = [\,m\le k\,]$ for all $m$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By induction the $m$-th residue iterate of $0$ is $\min(m,k+1)$; the verdict
there is $[\min(m,k+1)\le k] = [m\le k]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp depth hierarchy]\label{thm:depth}
For every $k$ there is a test on $n = k+2$ dishes and a dish $d$ with
$T(j) = T(0)$ for all $j\le k = n-2$ and $T(n-1)\neq T(0)$. Combined with
Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid}: on $n$ dishes every destruction depth $< n$ is realised
and no depth $\ge n$ is. The horizon $n$ is exact.
\end{theorem}

Write $t^{\langle m\rangle}$ for the \emph{batch} ``run $t$ exactly $m$ times,
accepting iff every run accepted'': $t^{\langle 0\rangle} = \mathbf 1$ and
$t^{\langle m+1\rangle} = t^{\langle m\rangle}\cdot t$. Then
$r_{t^{\langle m\rangle}} = r_t\iter{m}$ and $v_{t^{\langle m\rangle}}(d) = \tru$
iff $T_{t,d}(j) = \tru$ for all $j < m$.

\begin{theorem}[Finite testing certifies infinite testing]\label{thm:forever}
Let $|D| = n$. If $v_{t^{\langle n\rangle}}(d) = \tru$ then
$v_{t^{\langle m\rangle}}(d) = \tru$ for every $m$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:depth}
the constant $n$ cannot be replaced by $n-1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from the batch verdict formula and Lemma~\ref{lem:orbit}.
\end{proof}

\section{Stabilisation: destruction is confined to the transient}

\begin{lemma}[Idempotent iterate]\label{lem:idem}
For any self-map $f$ of a finite set there is $N > 0$ with
$f\iter{N}\circ f\iter{N} = f\iter{N}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The sequence $(f\iter{m})_m$ takes finitely many values, so $f\iter{i} =
f\iter{j}$ for some $i < j$; put $p = j-i>0$. Then $f\iter{m+p} = f\iter{m}$ for
$m\ge i$, hence $f\iter{m+cp} = f\iter{m}$ for all $c$ and $m\ge i$. Take
$N = p(i+1)$: then $N \ge i$ and $N+N = N+(i+1)p$, so $f\iter{2N} = f\iter{N}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Stabilisation]\label{thm:stab}
For every test $t$ on a finite dish type there is $N > 0$ with
$r_{t^{\langle N\rangle}}\circ r_{t^{\langle N\rangle}} =
r_{t^{\langle N\rangle}}$. Consequently $t^{\langle N\rangle}$ is
nondestructive on its own image.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Reversibility on the core]\label{thm:core}
For every test $t$ on a finite dish type there is $N > 0$ such that $r_t$
restricts to a bijection of the stabilised core
$C = \mathrm{range}(r_t\iter{N})$ onto itself.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $N = M+1$ idempotent as in Lemma~\ref{lem:idem} and write $f = r_t$.
Every $x\in C$ satisfies $f\iter{N}(x) = x$. Then $f$ maps $C$ into $C$ because
$f$ commutes with its iterates; $f$ is injective on $C$ because $f\iter{M}$ is a
left inverse there, $f\iter{M}(f(x)) = f\iter{N}(x) = x$; and $f$ is surjective
onto $C$ because $f\iter{M}(x)\in C$ and $f(f\iter{M}(x)) = f\iter{N}(x) = x$.
\end{proof}

Interpretation: whatever a test destroys, it destroys on the way in. This is the
mathematics of burn-in and pre-conditioning.

\section{Which verdict streams are realisable?}

\begin{theorem}[Analysis]\label{thm:analysis}
Let $|D| = n$. Every transcript $T_{t,d}$ is eventually periodic: there are
$i\ge 0$ and $p>0$ with $i+p\le n$ and $T_{t,d}(m+p) = T_{t,d}(m)$ for all
$m\ge i$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take the recurrence $r_t\iter{i+p}(d) = r_t\iter{i}(d)$ with $i+p\le n$ from the
proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:orbit}. A recurrence propagates forward: writing
$m+p = (m-i)+(i+p)$ gives $f\iter{m+p}(d) = f\iter{m}(d)$ for all $m\ge i$.
Apply $v_t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Rho test]
Let $i\ge 0$, $p>0$, $u : \N\to\B$. On $\{0,\dots,i+p-1\}$ define the
\textbf{rho map} $\rho(j) = j+1$ if $j+1 < i+p$ and $\rho(j) = i$ otherwise ---
a tail of length $i$ feeding a cycle of length $p$ --- and the \textbf{rho test}
$\rho_u(j) = (u(j), \rho(j))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Synthesis]\label{thm:synthesis}
If $u(m+p) = u(m)$ for all $m\ge i$, then $T_{\rho_u,\,0}(m) = u(m)$ for every
$m$: the rho test on $i+p$ dishes realises $u$ exactly.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $\iota(m)$ be the position reached after $m$ steps from $0$, so $\iota(0)=0$
and $\iota(m+1) = \iota(m)+1$ if that is $< i+p$, else $i$. By induction
$\iota(m) < i+p$, $m = \iota(m)+cp$ for some $c\ge 0$, and either $\iota(m) = m$
or $\iota(m)\ge i$. In the first case $u(\iota(m)) = u(m)$ trivially; in the
second, shifting the eventual periodicity by $c$ periods gives
$u(\iota(m)+cp) = u(\iota(m))$, i.e.\ $u(m) = u(\iota(m))$. The transcript at
step $m$ is $u(\iota(m))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[State-complexity duality]\label{thm:duality}
A Boolean stream $u$ is the transcript of some test on a dish type of size at
most $n$ if and only if $u$ is eventually periodic with
$\text{preperiod}+\text{period}\le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
A stream is realisable on a one-dish type iff it is constant. Constant
transcripts are exactly complexity $1$, the minimum: certificates sit at the
bottom of the scale.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Strict hierarchy]\label{thm:hierarchy}
For $n\ge 1$ let $u_n(m) = [\,n\nmid m\,]$. Then $u_n$ is realisable on $n$
dishes but on no fewer.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$u_n$ has preperiod $0$ and period $n$. Conversely suppose $u_n$ is eventually
$p$-periodic from index $i$ with $i+p\le n-1$. Evaluating at $m = ni \ge i$:
$n\mid ni$ so $u_n(ni) = \fls$, hence $u_n(ni+p) = \fls$, hence $n\mid ni+p$,
hence $n\mid p$, hence $p\ge n$, contradicting $p\le n-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Certificates cannot simulate destruction]\label{thm:nosim}
There is a test on two dishes whose transcript from a suitable dish is matched
by no nondestructive test whatsoever.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $\fuse_0$ started at $0$: by Lemma~\ref{lem:fuse} its transcript is
$\tru,\fls,\fls,\dots$, not constant, whereas every certificate has constant
transcript by Theorem~\ref{thm:nd-rep}.
\end{proof}

\section{How many runs distinguish two dishes?}

\begin{definition}
Dishes $d, e$ are \textbf{observationally equivalent} for $t$ if
$T_{t,d}(m) = T_{t,e}(m)$ for all $m$.
\end{definition}

Running $t$ on the pair simultaneously is a dynamical system on $D\times D$ with
$n^2$ states, and pigeonhole gives a bound of $n^2$ runs. That is correct and
wasteful. The improvement is a genuine cross-domain import: the
state-transition side supplies eventual periodicity with $i+p\le n$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:analysis}); the word-combinatorial side supplies the
\emph{Fine--Wilf periodicity lemma}, that a finite word with periods $p$ and $q$
and length at least $p+q-\gcd(p,q)$ has period $\gcd(p,q)$.

\begin{lemma}[Fine--Wilf for streams]\label{lem:fw}
Let $s : \N\to\alpha$ be globally $p$-periodic and $q$-periodic on the window
$[0, p+q-\gcd(p,q))$. Then $s(m) = s(m \bmod \gcd(p,q))$ for all $m$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Form the word $w$ of length $L = p+q-\gcd(p,q)$ with $j$-th letter $s(j)$. The
hypotheses give $w$ periods $p$ and $q$, so Fine--Wilf gives it period
$g = \gcd(p,q)$. Hence $s(k) = s(k\bmod g)$ for all $k<p$ by descending in steps
of $g$ inside the window; combined with $s(m) = s(m \bmod p)$ and $g\mid p$ this
gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Distinguishing engine]\label{lem:engine}
Suppose $T_{t,d}$ is $p_1$-periodic from index $i_1$, $T_{t,e}$ is
$p_2$-periodic from index $i_2$, and the two agree on all indices $j < T$ where
$T \ge \max(i_1,i_2)+p_1+p_2-\gcd(p_1,p_2)$. Then they agree at every index.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Shift both streams by $I = \max(i_1,i_2)$, making them globally $p_1$- and
$p_2$-periodic. On the window of agreement each inherits the other's period;
Lemma~\ref{lem:fw} applied twice shows both shifted streams are determined by
their values on $[0,g)$, $g = \gcd(p_1,p_2)$. Those values lie inside the window
of agreement, so the streams coincide above $I$; below $I$ they coincide by
hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Linear bound]\label{thm:2n}
If $T_{t,d}(j) = T_{t,e}(j)$ for all $j < 2n$, then $T_{t,d} = T_{t,e}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[Minimal recurrence]\label{lem:minrec}
For $f : D\to D$ finite and $d\in D$ there are $i\ge0$, $p>0$ with
$f\iter{i+p}(d) = f\iter{i}(d)$ such that $f\iter{0}(d),\dots,f\iter{i+p-1}(d)$
are pairwise distinct; hence $i+p\le n$. Moreover $p$ divides every eventual
period of the orbit.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Minimise $i+p$; minimality forces distinctness, and distinctness bounds $i+p$ by
$|D|$. For divisibility, the shifted orbit $m\mapsto f\iter{i+m}(d)$ is
genuinely $p$-periodic hence determined by residues mod $p$; with
$M = i + p(J+1)\ge J$ one computes $f\iter{M}(d) = f\iter{i}(d)$ and
$f\iter{M+q}(d) = f\iter{\,i+(q\bmod p)\,}(d)$, so the eventual period hypothesis
gives $f\iter{\,i+(q\bmod p)\,}(d) = f\iter{i}(d)$. If $q\bmod p \neq 0$ this
contradicts distinctness.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Orbit dichotomy]\label{lem:dich}
With minimal recurrence data $(i_1,p_1)$ for $d$ and $(i_2,p_2)$ for $e$ under
$f = r_t$: either the orbits never meet, in which case their point sets are
disjoint and $(i_1+p_1)+(i_2+p_2)\le n$; or they meet, in which case
$p_1 = p_2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
In the first case the two orbits contribute $i_1+p_1$ and $i_2+p_2$ distinct
dishes and are disjoint. In the second, if $f\iter{a}(d) = f\iter{b}(e)$ the
orbits agree after a shift, so $p_1$ is an eventual period of the orbit of $e$
and $p_2$ of the orbit of $d$; the divisibility clause of
Lemma~\ref{lem:minrec} gives $p_2\mid p_1$ and $p_1\mid p_2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpened indistinguishability]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $|D| = n$. If $T_{t,d}(j) = T_{t,e}(j)$ for all $j < n$, then
$T_{t,d} = T_{t,e}$: observational equivalence is decided by $n$ runs.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:engine}; it suffices to bound the window
$W = \max(i_1,i_2)+p_1+p_2-\gcd(p_1,p_2)$ by $n$ using minimal recurrence data.
In the disjoint case $(i_1+p_1)+(i_2+p_2)\le n$, and since
$\max(i_1,i_2)\le i_1+i_2$ and $\gcd\ge1$ we get $W\le n$. In the meeting case
$p_1 = p_2 = p$ and $\gcd(p_1,p_2) = p$, so $W = \max(i_1,i_2)+p \le
\max(i_1+p,\, i_2+p)\le n$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:minrec}.
\end{proof}

One number, $n$, governs both horizons: after $n$ runs a transcript can no
longer change its mind, and after $n$ runs two dishes can no longer part
company. Delay is nevertheless real.

\begin{definition}[Clock test]
On $\{0,1,2,3,4\}$ let the residue map be the permutation with cycles $(0\,1)$
and $(2\,3\,4)$, and let the verdicts at $0,\dots,4$ be
$\tru,\fls,\tru,\fls,\tru$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Distinguishing delay]\label{thm:clock}
For the clock test, dishes $0$ and $2$ have transcripts agreeing at steps
$0,1,2$ and disagreeing at step $3$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Direct computation: from $0$ the transcript is $\tru,\fls,\tru,\fls,\dots$ with
period $2$; from $2$ it is $\tru,\fls,\tru,\tru,\dots$ with period $3$.
\end{proof}

The clock test is exactly the Fine--Wilf extremal configuration: coprime periods
$2$ and $3$, requiring a window of $2+3-1 = 4 = n-1$.

\section{Algorithms}

All of the above is effective on finite dish types.

\emph{Classification.} Given a test as a table of $n$ pairs, decide
nondestructiveness by $n$ comparisons, reversibility by checking whether the
residue column is a permutation, repeatability by $n$ verdict comparisons; total
$O(n)$.

\emph{Destruction depth.} Iterate the residue map, recording verdicts, until the
verdict first differs from the initial one or $n$ steps elapse; by
Theorem~\ref{thm:rigid} the answer after $n$ steps is definitive. $O(n)$.

\emph{Orbit decomposition.} The minimal recurrence $(i,p)$ of a dish is computed
in $O(n)$ time and space by walking the orbit with a first-visit timestamp
array: the first repeated dish occurs at index $i+p$ and its first occurrence at
index $i$. This yields the exact state complexity $i+p$ of the transcript.

\emph{Observational equivalence.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}, compare the first
$n$ transcript entries; $O(n)$, with no fixed-point iteration, because the
residue map is a deterministic self-map --- a functional graph rather than a
general automaton.

\emph{Realisation.} Given a target eventually periodic stream, the rho test on
$i+p$ dishes realises it and no smaller dish type does when $(i,p)$ is minimal;
construction is $O(i+p)$.

\section{Applications}

\emph{Laboratory protocol design.} Theorems~\ref{thm:comm}
and~\ref{thm:noncomm} formalise the rule ``do the nondestructive assays first''
and explain why it is needed: a battery containing a destructive test has an
order-dependent verdict. Theorem~\ref{thm:restorable} says a destructive step
can be tolerated in any position exactly when it admits a restoring follow-up.

\emph{Acceptance sampling and burn-in.} Theorem~\ref{thm:forever} is a stopping
rule: $n$ consecutive passing runs certify all future runs, and $n-1$ do not
suffice. Theorems~\ref{thm:stab} and~\ref{thm:core} formalise burn-in.

\emph{Measurement models.} $\readflip$ models an information-preserving but
disturbing measurement; $\burn$ models one stable precisely because maximally
destructive. Theorem~\ref{thm:invariant} is the combinatorial analogue of the
statement that a repeatable measurement projects onto an invariant subspace.

\emph{Consumable credentials.} Theorem~\ref{thm:hierarchy} quantifies the state
cost of a prescribed usage pattern: realising a credential accepting on a
prescribed eventually periodic schedule needs exactly
$\text{preperiod}+\text{period}$ internal states.

\emph{Testing of stateful systems.} Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} is a conformance
bound; Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} the corresponding synthesis result.

\section{Discussion and open problems}

The development is purely structural: no hardness label is attached to any
class. The claims are that the classes are distinct pointwise
(Theorem~\ref{thm:strict}), distinct in closure properties
(Theorems~\ref{thm:closure}, \ref{thm:semilattice}, \ref{thm:rep-notclosed}),
distinct in realisable behaviours (Theorems~\ref{thm:duality},
\ref{thm:nosim}), and that the quantitative horizons are exactly $n$
(Theorems~\ref{thm:rigid}, \ref{thm:depth}, \ref{thm:sharp}).

The model is deterministic, single-generator, and structure-free. A
probabilistic test would return a distribution over $\B\times D$, and the
correct analogue of transcript rigidity is not obvious. An adaptive adversary
choosing among several tests corresponds to a transition monoid on more than one
generator, where functional-graph arguments give way to general automaton theory
and the linear bounds are expected to degrade.

Two plausible conjectures failed and are worth recording. Repeatable tests do
\emph{not} form a submonoid (Theorem~\ref{thm:rep-notclosed}). And
``$k$-repeatability for all dishes'' does \emph{not} give a strict hierarchy in
$k$: it collapses at $k=1$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:rep-iter}. The correct hierarchy
is the pointwise one of Theorem~\ref{thm:depth}, indexed by destruction depth at
a given dish.

Finally, exhaustive enumeration over small dish types indicates the true
distinguishing threshold is $n-1$ rather than $n$, with the clock test as the
extremal witness.

\begin{conjecture}[Sharp distinguishing threshold]
For every finite dish type with $n = |D| \ge 2$, every test $t$ and all dishes
$d, e$: if $T_{t,d}(j) = T_{t,e}(j)$ for all $j < n-1$, then
$T_{t,d} = T_{t,e}$. Equivalently, the maximal distinguishing delay is exactly
$n-2$.
\end{conjecture}

The route is visible in the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}. In the disjoint
case the slack is large and one expects to shave a unit from
$\max(i_1,i_2)\le\min(i_1+p_1, i_2+p_2)-1$. In the meeting case equality
$\max(i_1,i_2)+p = n$ would require one orbit to exhaust the dish space, which
is incompatible with the other orbit meeting it at a distinct preperiod. Making
both estimates uniform is the content of the conjecture.

Beyond it: extend to cost-bearing tests, where the residue carries a monotone
cost and one seeks cheapest batteries; study the transformation monoid generated
by several tests, where the natural question becomes the diameter of the
reachable state set; characterise which \emph{pairs} of streams are
simultaneously realisable on $n$ dishes, a two-orbit refinement of
Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} that is exactly what the conjecture needs; and develop
the probabilistic model.

